From 61c714e63402ed647ce1c1492424324813e69100 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 4 <phase4@openwch.local>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 07:02:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] RISC-V: add WCH XW compressed load and store forms

The official WCH 12.2 assembler emits the eight observed compressed byte and halfword load/store encodings under XW, accepts their ordinary-instruction aliases, preserves initial-architecture sticky eligibility, and shares the D+C encoding slot and diagnostics. Add those forms through the normal RISC-V opcode and compression paths.

Eligibility is decided once by the initial architecture. Only riscv_set_arch sets the flag, so `.option arch, +xw' does not turn it on, and the flag is deliberately not part of struct riscv_option_stack, so `.option pop' does not turn it back off.

The encodings collide with the compressed double-precision accesses -- MATCH_XW_C_LBU 0x2000/0xe003 is the c.fld slot -- so the disassembler decodes them only when asked, and objdump -M xw is what asks. This matches the official build in both directions: without the option both toolchains print .2byte for these halfwords, and with it both print the ordinary lbu/lhu/sb/sh mnemonics, including for an object that was assembled as plain D+C. The option is listed in objdump --help with the official wording.

The disassembler therefore gates the XW rows twice. The option decides whether they take part in matching at all, and once they do they are exempt from the architecture test: the official build decodes them under -M xw against an object whose architecture attribute never mentions XW, so the attribute cannot be what selects them, and riscv_multi_subset_supports has no INSN_CLASS_XW case to consult in any event. Which of several matching encodings wins is left to the table order, as for every other class, so the ordinary-alias rows keep their place ahead of the compressed ones.

WCH 12.2 behavior evidence: tmp/phase4-evidence/s3-xw-compressed-final/ and tmp/phase4-evidence/s3-xw-compressed-diagnostics/. Official default and -M xw disassembly sweep, covering all eight forms, their immediate ranges, no-aliases and numeric, plus the D+C and custom32 controls: tmp/phase8-evidence/12.2.0/probes/u3-spec-detail.log section 3. Encoding reference: ref/wch-isa-research/isa/custom/wch-custom-isa-reference.md section 3.1 for the slots and immediate mappings and section 3.2 for the D/C/Zcb exclusivity boundary.

The xlen_requirement column stays 0. The official 12.2 assembler takes all of these forms under -march=rv64imac_xw and emits the same halfwords as for rv32, and its objdump -M xw decodes them out of an elf64 object; pinning the column to 32 would diverge on four surfaces, the quietest of which is silent -- with the rows filtered out on rv64 the lbu/lhu/sb/sh aliases still assemble as their four-byte base forms and the object grows with no diagnostic. The rv64 acceptance, encoding, diagnostic and disassembly matrix measured against the WCH 12.2.0 binaries is in tmp/phase9-evidence/12.2.0/spec.md.
